Idea: same circuit as the best kernel (4 ct×ct multiplications, 5 plaintext multiplications), but the integer multiples are factored Horner-style with shared small shifts. Only the ciphertext additions change, and there are far fewer of them than with the binary expansion of each constant.
- t3 = 4(z³ − z) + z
- t5 = 4(4(z⁵ − z³) − z³) + 5z
- t7 = 8·A − 7z, where A = 2(4(z⁷ − 2z⁵) + z⁵) + 7z³

```cpp
Ciphertext dbl(const Ciphertext &c) { return c + c; }
Ciphertext m4(const Ciphertext &c) { return dbl(dbl(c)); }
Ciphertext m8(const Ciphertext &c) { return dbl(dbl(dbl(c))); }

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext s = square(z);
  Ciphertext z3 = s * z;
  Ciphertext z5 = s * z3;
  Ciphertext z7 = s * z5;
  Ciphertext z4 = m4(z);
  Ciphertext t3 = m4(z3 - z) + z;
  Ciphertext t5 = m4(m4(z5 - z3) - z3) + (z4 + z);
  Ciphertext A = dbl(m4(z7 - dbl(z5)) + z5) + (m8(z3) - z3);
  Ciphertext t7 = m8(A) - (m8(z) - z);
  Ciphertext result = z * c1;
  result = result + t3 * c3;
  result = result + t5 * c5;
  result = result + t7 * c7;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: group the terms by monomial, so all additions of one power of z happen at the same level and with the same multiplication history. Each monomial's total is c_k times the monomial, with integer multiples applied after the plaintext multiplication via additions. This uses more plaintext multiplications (10 instead of 5), but it should cut the many scale alignments that mixed-level sums force. The four monomial groups are joined at four different levels.

```cpp
Ciphertext imul(const Ciphertext &c, int n)
{
  Ciphertext acc;
  Ciphertext cur = c;
  bool has = false;
  while (n > 0)
  {
    if (n & 1)
    {
      if (has)
        acc = acc + cur;
      else
      {
        acc = cur;
        has = true;
      }
    }
    n >>= 1;
    if (n > 0)
      cur = cur + cur;
  }
  return acc;
}

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext s = square(z);
  Ciphertext z3 = s * z;
  Ciphertext z5 = s * z3;
  Ciphertext z7 = s * z5;

  // coefficient of z: c1 - 3c3 + 5c5 - 7c7
  Ciphertext g1 = (z * c1 + imul(z * c5, 5)) - (imul(z * c3, 3) + imul(z * c7, 7));
  // z^3: 4c3 - 20c5 + 56c7
  Ciphertext g3 = (imul(z3 * c3, 4) + imul(z3 * c7, 56)) - imul(z3 * c5, 20);
  // z^5: 16c5 - 112c7
  Ciphertext g5 = imul(z5 * c5, 16) - imul(z5 * c7, 112);
  // z^7: 64c7
  Ciphertext g7 = imul(z7 * c7, 64);

  Ciphertext result = g7 + g5;
  result = result + g3;
  result = result + g1;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: factor out z, so p = c0 + z·(Q(s) + constant part), where s = z².
- The constant part is z·(c1 − 3c3 + 5c5 − 7c7). I build it as four small plaintext multiplications on z, with the integer factors applied before the final add. The largest factor is only 7, which avoids the precision loss that broke attempt 6.
- The s-dependent part is c3·4s + c5·(16s² − 20s) + c7·(64s³ − 112s² + 56s). The integer combinations are built on ciphertexts before the plaintext multiplication, and the three terms land at different levels.
- One final z·W multiplication finishes the polynomial.

```cpp
Ciphertext imul(const Ciphertext &c, int n)
{
  Ciphertext acc;
  Ciphertext cur = c;
  bool has = false;
  while (n > 0)
  {
    if (n & 1)
    {
      if (has)
        acc = acc + cur;
      else
      {
        acc = cur;
        has = true;
      }
    }
    n >>= 1;
    if (n > 0)
      cur = cur + cur;
  }
  return acc;
}

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext s = square(z);
  Ciphertext s2 = square(s);
  Ciphertext s3 = s2 * s;

  Ciphertext A3 = imul(s, 4);
  Ciphertext A5 = imul(s2, 16) - imul(s, 20);
  Ciphertext A7 = (imul(s3, 64) + imul(s, 56)) - imul(s2, 112);

  Ciphertext W = A3 * c3;
  W = W + A5 * c5;
  W = W + A7 * c7;

  Ciphertext zc = (z * c1 + imul(z * c5, 5)) - (imul(z * c3, 3) + imul(z * c7, 7));

  Ciphertext result = z * W;
  result = result + zc;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: this keeps the attempt-4 chain z, s, z³, z⁵, z⁷ (4 ct×ct multiplications) and pulls all the z-linear parts out. The z coefficient c1 − 3c3 + 5c5 − 7c7 is built from four small plaintext multiplications on z, with integer factors up to 7 only. The remaining Chebyshev parts then use only z³, z⁵ and z⁷ with integer factors, which cuts both the additions and the cross-level alignments on the z terms.

